# NestJS Boilerplate — Project Context

## Product

Production-ready NestJS API starter with authentication, users, audit logging, email, optional Redis cache, and BullMQ background jobs. Use it as a foundation for new backend services.

## Scope boundaries

- Prefer modular monolith. Do not introduce microservices, CQRS, or event sourcing by default.
- Add domain modules under `src/modules/` as features grow.
- Keep shared infrastructure in `src/common/`, `src/config/`, `src/database/`, `src/integrations/`, and `src/jobs/`.

## Included modules

- `identity`: authentication, users, roles, sessions, tokens, password reset, email verification.
- `communication`: reminders, in-app notifications, delivery tracking.

## Technology stack

- NestJS 11.
- TypeScript strict mode.
- PostgreSQL.
- Drizzle ORM and Drizzle Kit.
- pnpm only.
- Redis and BullMQ (optional; disable via env when not needed).
- REST APIs under `/api`.
- OpenAPI/Swagger at `/api/docs`.
- JWT authentication with role-based access.
- Jest and Supertest.
- Structured logging.
- Docker Compose for local Postgres and Redis.

Never use npm, Yarn, Bun, Prisma, TypeORM, Sequelize, or Mongoose unless explicitly requested.

## Engineering priorities

1. Correct authorization.
2. Security.
3. Data integrity and auditability.
4. Reliability and observability.
5. Testability.
6. Maintainability.
7. Performance.
8. Delivery speed.

Fast delivery never justifies bypassing validation, authorization, transactions, tests, or migrations.

## General coding rules

- Code identifiers and technical documentation in English.
- Read existing code and `docs/` before proposing new patterns.
- Reuse shared utilities and conventions.
- Organize code by domain module under `src/modules`.
- Keep controllers thin; business rules in services.
- Database access in repositories or named persistence services.
- Validate transport input with DTOs (`class-validator` / `class-transformer`).
- Do not treat database models as automatic API contracts.
- Never expose sensitive fields from Drizzle rows.
- Never silently swallow errors. Avoid `any`.
- Never log secrets, tokens, passwords, cookies, or OTP values.
- Never trust user IDs, roles, or timestamps from request bodies for authorization.
- Use UTC internally. Return ISO 8601 timestamps.
- Use database constraints in addition to application validation.

## Package management

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm add <package>
pnpm add -D <package>
pnpm exec <binary>
pnpm run <script>
```

Keep `pnpm-lock.yaml` committed.

## Definition of done

- Business rule implemented.
- Auth enforced on protected endpoints.
- Input validation exists.
- Database constraints and indexes appropriate.
- Tests cover success, failure, and unauthorized access where relevant.
- Swagger updated when API surface changes.
- Migrations generated and reviewed when schema changes.
- `pnpm run lint` passes.
- `pnpm typecheck` and relevant tests pass when applicable.
